# Line endings.
#
# Git on Windows is commonly configured with `core.autocrlf=true`, which
# rewrites checked-out files to CRLF. For anything that only a Windows editor
# reads that is harmless. For a file executed inside a Linux container it is
# not: a CRLF script's shebang becomes `#!/bin/bash\r`, the kernel looks for an
# interpreter literally named `/bin/bash\r`, and the failure is reported as
#
#   Error while running script .../01-create-bucket.sh:
#   [Errno 2] No such file or directory
#
# which names the SCRIPT, not the missing interpreter — so it reads as a broken
# volume mount. That is what silently stopped the LocalStack init hook from
# creating the uploads bucket, surfacing hours later as "The specified bucket
# does not exist" on the first logo upload.
#
# Normalise to LF in the repository, and force LF on checkout for everything
# that crosses into a container or is executed by a POSIX shell.

* text=auto

*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf
docker-compose*.yml text eol=lf
*.bash      text eol=lf

# Prisma migrations are applied by the CLI, not a shell, but keeping them LF
# stops a checkout on Windows from showing every migration as modified.
*.sql       text eol=lf
